<details class="wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow"><summary>Summary</summary>
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p>md-preview is a command-line tool written in Rust that renders Markdown files directly in your terminal, applying syntax highlighting and formatting. It leverages&nbsp;<code>pulldown-cmark</code>&nbsp;for robust Markdown parsing and&nbsp;<code>termcolor</code>&nbsp;for rich, colored output.</p>
</blockquote>
</details>



<p>Tired of leaving your terminal to view a simple Markdown file? Meet&nbsp;<strong><code>md-preview</code></strong>, a powerful new command-line tool written in&nbsp;<strong>Rust</strong>&nbsp;that brings beautiful, formatted Markdown directly to your terminal screen. It&#8217;s designed to be fast, efficient, and highly readable, so you can focus on your work without context switching.</p>



<span id="more-102"></span>



<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Why&nbsp;<code>md-preview</code>?</h2>



<p>While many tools exist for viewing Markdown,&nbsp;<code>md-preview</code>&nbsp;is built specifically for the terminal environment. It uses&nbsp;<strong><code>pulldown-cmark</code></strong> for fast and accurate Markdown parsing and&nbsp;<strong><code>termcolor</code></strong>&nbsp;to apply rich syntax highlighting and formatting. This isn&#8217;t just a simple text dump; <code>md-preview</code>&nbsp;understands the structure of your document, rendering elements like headings, bold text, lists, and even tables with distinct colors and proper alignment.</p>



<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Key Features:</strong></h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Customizable</strong>: You can choose to show or hide the original Markdown symbols (like&nbsp;<code>#</code>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<code>**</code>) and even center the content in your terminal for better readability.</li>
</ul>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Syntax Highlighting</strong>: Easily distinguish between different Markdown elements at a glance.</li>
</ul>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Table Rendering</strong>: Tables are rendered with clean borders and proper alignment, making them easy to read.</li>
</ul>



<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Usage</h2>



<p>Once installed, you can use it right away:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>To render a file with default settings:</li>
</ul>



<pre class="wp-block-code" style="line-height:0"><code><code>md-preview README.md</code></code></pre>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>To show the original Markdown symbols:</li>
</ul>



<pre class="wp-block-code" style="line-height:0"><code><code>md-preview MyDocument.md --symbol</code></code></pre>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>To center the content for a more focused view:</li>
</ul>



<pre class="wp-block-code" style="line-height:0"><code><code>md-preview Notes.md --center 10</code></code></pre>



<p><code>md-preview</code>&nbsp;is perfect for anyone who works with Markdown files and wants a seamless, terminal-native viewing experience. It&#8217;s a testament to the power and performance of Rust.</p>



<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>



<p>I&#8217;d love for you to give it a try. Head over to the&nbsp;<strong>GitHub repository</strong>&nbsp;to get the code and start using&nbsp;<code>md-preview</code>&nbsp;today. Let me know what you think!</p>



